/** Expanded, Scripture-first teaching for the couple. Each day is completed together. */
export const marriageDeepLessons: Record<number, { setting: string; teaching: string[]; questions: string[]; prayer: string }> = {
  1: {
    setting: 'James 1:19–20 belongs to a letter addressed to scattered believers who faced trials. In the surrounding verses James contrasts human anger with the righteousness God desires, then calls readers to receive and obey the implanted word. Proverbs 18:13 warns against answering before listening. Neither text is exclusively about marriage; both confront the habit of speaking before understanding.',
    teaching: [
      'Imagine coming home exhausted after a day of serving other people. One spouse says, “I felt alone today.” The other hears, “You are a terrible spouse,” even though those words were never spoken. Within seconds the conversation becomes a defense of intentions rather than an effort to understand pain. The first person feels dismissed. The second feels accused. Neither has actually heard the other. This pattern can happen even when both people sincerely love God and want a healthy marriage.',
      'James begins with a responsibility each believer can practice: be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to anger. The order matters. Listening is not merely waiting until the other person stops talking. It means being curious enough to ask what they experienced, resisting the urge to correct every detail, and being willing to discover that your good intentions did not prevent a painful impact. Proverbs 18:13 calls it foolish to answer a matter before hearing it. You cannot wisely answer what you have not yet understood.',
      'Consider two responses to “Ministry seems to come before our family.” A defensive response is, “You know I am doing God’s work. Why are you attacking me?” A listening response is, “It sounds as though you have been carrying more at home than I realized. Can you tell me when that has felt hardest?” The second response does not settle the scheduling question or mean that every complaint is accurate. It simply creates room to learn what the concern actually is. Both spouses will still need to speak truthfully, take responsibility, and make practical decisions.',
      'Listening also has boundaries. James does not command anyone to remain in a threatening conversation, accept insults, or endure coercion. Either spouse may ask for a pause and return when it is safe. A pause should include a realistic time to resume; otherwise it can become avoidance or punishment. If one spouse fears retaliation, a joint communication exercise is not the right next step. Safety and qualified support take priority.',
      'For today, do not try to solve your entire marriage. Practice one small change: before explaining your side, summarize what you heard and ask, “Did I understand you?” Then listen to the correction without debating it. This is one way to honor Christ in an ordinary conversation. The goal is not to win a five-minute exercise. It is to become people who are increasingly truthful, patient, and willing to understand each other.',
    ],
    questions: ['When I bring up a concern, what response helps me feel heard?', 'What do I usually assume my spouse means when I feel criticized?', 'What is the difference between agreeing with someone and understanding them?', 'Can we name a recent low-stakes misunderstanding without assigning blame?', 'What words can we use when either of us needs a safe pause?', 'What one listening behavior will we practice tomorrow?'],
    prayer: 'Lord Jesus, You know what we mean and what we fail to say. Teach us to listen patiently, speak truthfully, and treat each other with dignity. Help us recognize defensiveness and make room for understanding. Amen.',
  },
  2: {
    setting: 'Proverbs 4:23 is part of a father’s wisdom instruction to guard the heart because life flows from it. In Hebrew thought the heart includes thought, desire, and decision, not merely emotion. Psalm 139:23–24 is a prayer asking God to examine the worshiper, not permission to interrogate someone else.',
    teaching: [
      'Sometimes a disagreement about dishes, schedules, or a missed message becomes much larger than the event itself. One spouse may hear a request as rejection; another may experience silence as abandonment. Past experiences can influence those reactions, but it is not wise to assume every strong feeling is caused by trauma. Fatigue, stress, pregnancy, recovery responsibilities, and unmet expectations can also shape how people respond.',
      'The biblical invitation is to pay attention to your own heart before making confident claims about your spouse’s motives. Guarding the heart is not hiding feelings or refusing vulnerability. It is recognizing that our thoughts and assumptions influence our words and decisions. The prayer of Psalm 139 is especially important: “Search me,” not “Show me everything wrong with my spouse.” Each person takes responsibility for their own reactions.',
      'A helpful practice is to separate three things: what happened, what I felt, and the story I told myself. “You did not answer my message” describes an event. “I felt worried” names an experience. “You do not care about me” is an interpretation that may or may not be true. When we present interpretations as facts, a spouse may feel falsely accused. Instead, we can ask what happened and explain the effect honestly.',
      'Neither spouse is required to disclose painful memories or explain every reaction. Recovery and trauma histories deserve care, privacy, and sometimes professional support. If one person needs a pause, agree on how to return to the conversation without leaving the other indefinitely uncertain. If fear or intimidation is present, stop the joint exercise and seek safe individual help.',
      'Today, practice humility before certainty. Ask God to reveal your assumptions and choose a response that gives your spouse the benefit of a question before the burden of an accusation. This is not pretending hurt does not matter. It is making room for truth.',
    ],
    questions: ['What ordinary situation tends to make me defensive?', 'How can I tell the difference between an event and my interpretation of it?', 'What helps me calm down without withdrawing as punishment?', 'How would I like my spouse to ask about a strong reaction?', 'What past experiences am I comfortable discussing, and what should remain private?', 'What pause-and-return plan feels respectful to both of us?'],
    prayer: 'Father, examine our hearts with mercy. Help us tell the truth about our feelings without assuming the worst about each other. Give us wisdom to recognize when we need rest, patience, or outside help. Amen.',
  },
  3: {
    setting: 'Ephesians 4:15, 25–32 is addressed to the church as a community being renewed in Christ. Paul calls believers to put away falsehood, speak truth, address anger without giving it destructive room, and use words that build up. Marriage is a fitting place to practice these commands, though it is not their only setting.',
    teaching: [
      'Two unhealthy approaches can look like opposites: saying every thought harshly because “I am just being honest,” and hiding every concern because “I do not want conflict.” Neither is the truthful love Paul describes. Truth without care can wound. Avoidance without honesty can allow resentment to grow. Christian speech calls us to hold truth and love together.',
      'Ephesians does not say that good communication means never being angry. It warns us not to let anger become a doorway to destructive behavior. A spouse can say, “I am upset that we agreed on a plan and it changed without telling me,” without attacking character: “You never care about anyone but yourself.” The first statement names an observable issue. The second assigns a sweeping identity and makes repair harder.',
      'When a couple has demanding ministry commitments and children who need dependable care, clarity matters. “I need you to be more present” may be sincere but vague. “Can we put our phones away for twenty minutes after dinner on three nights this week?” is something both can understand and evaluate. Specific requests invite practical cooperation. They also expose when a request is unrealistic and needs renegotiation.',
      'Speaking with grace does not require silence about mistreatment. Threats, humiliation, spiritual manipulation, and repeated intimidation are not ordinary communication differences. The harmed person is not responsible for finding a perfect tone that makes someone else safe. If those behaviors occur, stop the joint exercise and seek appropriate help.',
      'Today, practice a truthful opening that neither attacks nor hides. Describe the situation, name your experience, and ask for one concrete change. Then allow your spouse to respond honestly. The goal is not a flawless script; it is a growing habit of truthful, compassionate speech.',
    ],
    questions: ['Do I tend to speak harshly or avoid difficult subjects?', 'What makes a concern specific rather than accusatory?', 'How can I tell my spouse the truth without labeling their character?', 'What practical request have I left vague?', 'How should we respond when a request is reasonable but cannot be met immediately?', 'What would repair sound like after hurtful words?'],
    prayer: 'Jesus, make our words truthful and gracious. Forgive the ways we have attacked or withdrawn. Teach us to speak clearly, listen humbly, and repair harm with changed actions. Amen.',
  },
  4: {
    setting: 'Proverbs 15:1 is a wisdom saying about the power of a gentle answer to turn away wrath. Romans 12:18 belongs to Paul’s instruction about sincere love and living peaceably with others, and includes the important qualification “if possible, so far as it depends on you.” Neither passage says that one person can prevent every conflict.',
    teaching: [
      'A disagreement often becomes a battle when both spouses stop discussing the original problem and begin protecting themselves. A change in the calendar becomes “You never care.” A forgotten task becomes “I cannot depend on you for anything.” The words “always” and “never” make one incident feel like a verdict on the whole relationship. At that point, each person may be fighting to prove their own goodness rather than seeking a workable answer.',
      'Proverbs does not teach that a soft voice magically fixes anger. A gentle answer is a wise response that refuses to add needless heat. It can still be direct: “I want to talk about this, but I will not continue while we are insulting each other.” A gentle answer does not mean surrendering a boundary, ignoring wrongdoing, or pretending an issue is small. It means choosing words that make understanding more possible.',
      'Paul adds a necessary limit: live peaceably if possible, as far as it depends on you. You can control your own speech and conduct, but you cannot control whether another person is honest, safe, or willing to listen. This distinction protects couples from the false idea that if one spouse were more patient, the other would never mistreat them. Christian responsibility is personal; it is not unlimited responsibility for another person’s behavior.',
      'Try naming the actual issue before discussing it: “We both agreed to be home by six, but a ministry call changed our evening. How should we handle that next time?” If your heart is racing or you feel unable to listen, say, “I want to work this out. Can we pause for twenty minutes and return at seven?” The return time matters. A pause used to calm down is different from withdrawing to punish.',
      'Practice with a minor disagreement, not a frightening or high-conflict situation. Both spouses must be free to stop. If threats, intimidation, or retaliation occur, do not try to fix the situation with a better communication script. Seek qualified individual support. In ordinary disagreements, however, a couple can grow by choosing one issue, one calm opening, and one concrete next step.',
    ],
    questions: ['What is the first sign that a disagreement is escalating?', 'Which phrases make us feel attacked rather than understood?', 'How is a gentle answer different from avoiding an issue?', 'What responsibilities belong to me even if my spouse responds poorly?', 'What pause phrase and return time would help both of us?', 'What small disagreement can we practice resolving safely today?'],
    prayer: 'God of peace, help us speak without contempt and listen without fear. Give us courage to address what matters, wisdom to pause when needed, and humility to own our conduct. Amen.',
  },
  5: {
    setting: 'Philippians 2:3–4 calls believers to reject selfish ambition and consider the interests of others in light of Christ’s humility. First Corinthians 13 describes love within Paul’s wider correction of a divided church. Neither text makes one spouse responsible for excusing harmful conduct.',
    teaching: [
      '“I did not mean it that way” may be completely true. Yet it does not answer the question of how your words or choices affected your spouse. A missed promise might come from exhaustion rather than disregard, but the person waiting still experienced disappointment. A ministry obligation may have been accepted with generous intentions, but the resulting workload at home may still be unfair. Marriage requires room for both the explanation and the impact.',
      'Philippians does not ask us to deny our own needs. It asks us to notice the interests of others rather than treating our perspective as the only one that matters. Christ’s humility, described in the following verses, gives the passage its direction. We are not called to self-erasure or to being controlled by another person; we are called to love that does not insist on being the center of every conversation.',
      'Suppose one spouse says, “When you answered your phone during dinner, I felt like our family time did not matter.” A defensive reply might be, “It was important ministry business.” A more constructive reply is, “I thought the call was urgent. I can see that leaving the table hurt you. Next time I will tell the caller when I can respond, unless it is an actual emergency.” This response explains the intention without using it to erase the impact.',
      'An apology has several parts: naming the specific action, acknowledging its effect, taking responsibility without blaming the other person’s sensitivity, and identifying a realistic change. It does not require pretending you intended harm when you did not. Nor should an apology be demanded as a performance. If the issue is repeated or serious, trust is rebuilt by consistent conduct over time rather than by eloquent words.',
      'For today, practice with a small, recent misunderstanding that both spouses feel safe discussing. Each person should be allowed to describe their experience without interruption. Ask, “What did I miss?” and “What would help next time?” The goal is to become more attentive, not to decide which spouse has the better memory.',
    ],
    questions: ['When do I use my intentions to dismiss someone’s experience?', 'Can I explain my actions without making an excuse?', 'What is the difference between responsibility and accepting blame for something I did not do?', 'How does Christ’s humility challenge my usual response?', 'What would a specific, credible apology sound like?', 'What change would make an apology meaningful over time?'],
    prayer: 'Lord Jesus, You showed humility in action. Teach us to take responsibility for our choices, honor one another’s experience, and make repairs that can be seen in everyday life. Amen.',
  },
  6: {
    setting: 'Romans 12:10 urges believers to show family-like affection and honor one another within the church. First Peter 3:7 specifically instructs husbands to live with their wives in an understanding way and treat them with honor. These passages encourage attentive care rather than stereotypes about what every husband or wife needs.',
    teaching: [
      'People often offer the kind of care they personally appreciate. One spouse may show love by solving problems; the other may want someone to sit and listen. One may find comfort in a hug, while the other needs quiet after an overwhelming day. Neither response is automatically wrong. Trouble begins when we assume that because our effort was sincere, our spouse must experience it as helpful.',
      'Romans 12 calls Christians to honor one another. Honor means paying attention to the actual person rather than an imagined version of them. Peter’s instruction to husbands to live with understanding is especially practical: understanding must be learned. It requires asking questions, remembering answers, and adjusting behavior. It is not permission to claim authority over a spouse’s emotions or decisions.',
      'A household with children, recovery-related responsibilities, ministry demands, and an approaching birth may need different forms of care from week to week. A spouse who once appreciated an evening outing may now need uninterrupted rest. A spouse who usually handles transportation may need help when a schedule changes. Needs can change without either person being inconsistent or ungrateful.',
      'Try asking three simple questions: “What has felt heaviest this week?” “Would listening, practical help, or quiet company be most useful?” and “What is one thing I could do that would make tomorrow easier?” Do not promise more than you can deliver. If the request is beyond your capacity, say so kindly and work together on another solution. Reliability matters more than dramatic gestures.',
      'Mutual care also respects consent and boundaries. Neither person owes physical affection, disclosure of painful experiences, or immediate emotional availability to prove love. Care may mean accepting a respectful no. Today, choose one small action your spouse actually asked for, then follow through without expecting praise or a particular reaction.',
    ],
    questions: ['What kind of support helps me most when I am stressed?', 'What care do I tend to offer without checking whether it is wanted?', 'What need has changed in this season of our family?', 'How can we ask for help without criticizing each other?', 'What is one request we can realistically meet this week?', 'How will we respond graciously when a request cannot be met?'],
    prayer: 'Father, teach us to honor the person You have placed beside us. Give us curiosity, patience, and wisdom to offer the kind of care that truly helps. Amen.',
  },
  7: {
    setting: 'Colossians 3:12–15 calls God’s people to put on compassion, kindness, humility, patience, forgiveness, and love. Ephesians 4:29 calls for speech that builds others up according to need. A written communication covenant is a practical application of these virtues, not a biblical requirement or a substitute for accountability.',
    teaching: [
      'A good week of conversation does not mean a couple will never argue again. Growth is measured by the habits they practice when they are tired, disappointed, or misunderstood. That is why a communication covenant can help. Instead of waiting for a heated moment to decide what is acceptable, a couple agrees ahead of time how they want to treat one another.',
      'Colossians begins with an identity rooted in God’s grace: believers are chosen and loved, and therefore are to clothe themselves with compassion and humility. The passage is not a checklist for judging which spouse is the more spiritual one. It invites each person to take responsibility for embodying Christlike character. Love binds these virtues together; it does not cancel truth or boundaries.',
      'A useful covenant should be short, concrete, and mutual. For example: “We will not threaten, insult, mock, or use Scripture to shame each other.” “We will ask for clarification before assuming motives.” “Either person may request a pause, and we will agree on when to revisit the issue.” “When we cause harm, we will acknowledge it and name a practical repair.” Avoid promises such as “We will never be angry,” because anger itself is not the same as harmful behavior.',
      'Discuss how to handle broken agreements. A covenant without repair can become another source of accusation: “You broke rule three.” Instead, ask, “What happened? What responsibility do I need to take? What change will prevent this from repeating?” A pattern of intimidation or coercion requires more than a communication agreement. Safety, accountability, and qualified support come first.',
      'At your weekly meeting with your mentor, bring only the parts of the covenant you both freely choose to discuss. Your private reflections are not required. Ask for help identifying one habit to practice during the next seven days. The goal is not to perform a successful marriage for someone else. It is to build a home where truthful speech, compassion, and responsible repair are increasingly normal.',
    ],
    questions: ['Which communication habit improved this week?', 'When did we still interrupt, withdraw, or assume the worst?', 'What behaviors must be outside the boundaries of our disagreements?', 'What does a respectful pause and return look like for us?', 'How will we repair a broken commitment without using the covenant as a weapon?', 'What one practice will we bring into the next week?'],
    prayer: 'Christ, clothe us with compassion, humility, patience, and love. Help us keep the commitments we make, acknowledge harm without excuses, and seek wise support when we need it. Amen.',
  },
  8: {
    setting: 'Genesis 2:18–25 describes God forming the woman as a suitable partner for the man and the two becoming one flesh. Jesus quotes this creation account in Matthew 19:4–6 while answering a question about divorce. The passages establish marriage as a serious covenant, not a claim that spouses lose their personhood or safety.',
    teaching: [
      'Two people may exchange vows while still living with separate habits, calendars, assumptions, and loyalties. Becoming one flesh is not instant agreement about every decision. It means learning to share a life faithfully, where choices about time, money, children, service, and rest affect both spouses. Unity takes deliberate practice, especially when a marriage is new and the household already carries significant responsibilities.',
      'Genesis describes the woman as a corresponding helper, not an inferior assistant. The account presents human companionship as good and the marriage bond as a new primary family commitment. Jesus treats this union seriously. Neither passage licenses control, isolation from healthy support, or pressure to tolerate abuse. A covenant calls both spouses to faithfulness and responsibility.',
      'Consider a ministry invitation that arrives while one spouse is preparing dinner and caring for children. The invited spouse may feel called to help, while the other anticipates another evening carrying the household alone. Neither perspective should be dismissed. The question is not simply “Can I go?” but “What does accepting this commitment mean for our shared life, and what decision can we freely support together?”',
      'Shared decision-making needs a process. State the decision, let each spouse name hopes and concerns, identify who will carry the practical consequences, and consider realistic alternatives. Sometimes the answer is yes with a backup plan. Sometimes it is not now. A good decision respects the family’s actual capacity rather than the image of a ministry family that never says no.',
      'Today choose one small decision to make together. Do not use the exercise to pressure agreement about a major unresolved conflict. Practice saying, “Your perspective matters because this is our life.” The purpose is not to erase differences; it is to learn faithful partnership.',
    ],
    questions: ['Where do we still make decisions as though we are single?', 'What does becoming one flesh mean without losing our individual dignity?', 'Which household responsibility is easy for one spouse to overlook?', 'How can we make decisions without one person dominating?', 'What alternatives exist when both of our concerns are legitimate?', 'What one upcoming decision can we approach as a team?'],
    prayer: 'Creator God, thank You for the gift and responsibility of marriage. Teach us to seek unity without control, honor each other’s dignity, and make wise decisions for our shared home. Amen.',
  },
  9: {
    setting: 'Ephesians 5:25–33 calls husbands to self-giving love in the context of Paul’s teaching about Christ and the church. Mark 7:9–13 records Jesus rebuking religious traditions that allowed people to neglect their parents. Applying Mark 7 to modern ministry obligations is an analogy, not the passage’s direct subject.',
    teaching: [
      'A person can sincerely love serving others and still become unavailable to the people at home. Ministry emergencies may feel urgent because someone is calling, texting, or asking for prayer. A spouse or child may stop asking for help because they assume the answer will be “not now.” That silence can hide an increasing burden. Serving God does not require ignoring those entrusted to our daily care.',
      'Jesus condemned a religious excuse that made neglect appear holy. His specific example concerned financial support for parents, but the underlying warning is useful: religious language must not be used to avoid ordinary obligations. Ephesians describes love through Christ’s self-giving example, not through entitlement to be served. A husband cannot appeal to spiritual leadership to demand that his wife carry every cost of his outside ministry.',
      'The practical question is not whether all outside service should stop. It is whether the family can freely and realistically support the commitments being made. Some seasons allow more travel or evening work; other seasons require reduced availability. Pregnancy, childbirth, recovery meetings, and the needs of multiple children may change what is sustainable. Flexibility can be an act of faithfulness rather than a lack of devotion.',
      'Look at the calendar together. Mark essential family responsibilities, sleep, meals, and time for connection before adding optional ministry activities. Identify which requests are true emergencies and who can respond when you cannot. A ministry without backup support can quietly become dependent on one family’s exhaustion. Building a team protects both the household and the people being served.',
      'Avoid keeping score about whose service is more important. Instead ask what each spouse and each child needs to thrive this week. Then make one visible change. A protected dinner or a delegated phone call may be small, but reliable presence teaches love in ways that public service cannot replace.',
    ],
    questions: ['When does ministry feel urgent even when it can wait?', 'How does our current schedule affect each spouse and the children?', 'What is the difference between a genuine emergency and a request for immediate attention?', 'What responsibilities have we assumed the other person will handle?', 'Who can serve as a backup ministry contact?', 'What protected family time will we honor this week?'],
    prayer: 'Lord Jesus, keep our service rooted in love rather than guilt or the need to be needed. Show us how to care faithfully for our family and serve others with wise limits. Amen.',
  },
  10: {
    setting: 'Mark 10:42–45 records Jesus contrasting the domination of worldly rulers with servant leadership among His disciples. John 13:1–17 shows Jesus washing His disciples’ feet as an example of humble service. Both passages concern discipleship broadly; applying them to marriage must preserve mutual dignity and reject coercion.',
    teaching: [
      'Many couples hear the word leadership and picture someone making final decisions. Jesus gives a different picture: a leader who serves. In Mark 10, He rejects the habit of using position to rule over others. In John 13, He takes the low place and washes feet. The lesson is not that one spouse should do every chore or never express a need. It is that love does not use power to demand privilege.',
      'In an ordinary home, service can look unremarkable. One spouse notices that the other has been up with a child and prepares breakfast. One handles a phone call so the other can rest. Both ask which task is most useful rather than choosing a grand gesture that wins attention. These actions matter because they relieve actual burdens.',
      'Service becomes distorted when it is used to purchase affection, demand gratitude, or avoid accountability. “After everything I did for you, you owe me” turns a gift into leverage. Likewise, Scripture about service must never be used to pressure a spouse into silence, sex, unsafe reconciliation, or obedience to harmful demands. Jesus’ example confronts domination rather than justifying it.',
      'Ask each other what would genuinely help this week. The answer may surprise you. Someone who feels overwhelmed may need a quiet hour more than a date night. Someone who feels distant may need ten uninterrupted minutes of conversation rather than another completed chore. Honor the answer even if it is not what you would have chosen.',
      'Today each spouse should choose one practical act of service that has been requested or welcomed. Do it without announcing a sacrifice or expecting a particular response. Then discuss what you learned about the difference between helping someone and trying to manage them.',
    ],
    questions: ['What does Jesus reject about the way people use authority?', 'How does His example challenge our assumptions about leadership?', 'What practical burden could I lighten this week?', 'When have I used a good deed to seek recognition or leverage?', 'How can I ask whether help is wanted rather than assuming?', 'What does mutual service look like when both of us are exhausted?'],
    prayer: 'Jesus, You came to serve. Free us from the need to control or impress. Teach us to honor one another through humble, practical care. Amen.',
  },
  11: {
    setting: 'Philippians 2:3–4 urges Christians to attend to others’ interests with humility. Proverbs 20:5 compares the purposes of a person’s heart to deep waters that understanding can draw out. Neither passage promises that a spouse can read the other person’s mind.',
    teaching: [
      'Unspoken expectations can feel like obvious rules: one person assumes bills will be discussed before spending; another assumes each person can make small purchases independently. One expects regular contact during ministry events; the other sees a busy evening as a reason not to check the phone. Neither may have said the expectation aloud. When the rule is broken, the hurt can feel intentional even though the rule was never agreed upon.',
      'Humility begins by admitting that our normal is not necessarily our spouse’s normal. Family upbringing, previous relationships, recovery experiences, and current pressures can shape expectations. Some expectations are reasonable; others may be unrealistic or controlling. The answer is not to approve every preference, but to bring assumptions into the open and evaluate them together.',
      'Proverbs encourages understanding that draws out what is beneath the surface. Try asking, “What did you expect would happen?” before asking, “Why are you upset?” This question may reveal that the disagreement is about reliability, fairness, or feeling included rather than the immediate event. Understanding does not require agreement, but it helps you negotiate the actual issue.',
      'Choose one category: chores, money, parenting, visitors, rest, or ministry. Each spouse writes down what they expected, what they can realistically contribute, and what would feel fair. Compare the answers without mocking differences. If you disagree, create a one-week trial instead of demanding a permanent solution on the spot.',
      'For blended families, expectations about discipline and relationships with children deserve special patience. Children should not be pressured to show instant attachment or use particular family titles. Decisions that affect them should be thoughtful, age-appropriate, and consistent. Today’s goal is to replace mind-reading with a respectful conversation.',
    ],
    questions: ['What expectation did I assume was obvious?', 'Where did that expectation come from?', 'Which expectations are preferences, and which involve genuine responsibilities?', 'What household task feels uneven right now?', 'How can we negotiate without making one spouse the winner?', 'What one-week experiment could help us learn what works?'],
    prayer: 'Father, give us humility to ask rather than assume. Help us speak our needs clearly, listen without ridicule, and build agreements that are fair and realistic. Amen.',
  },
  12: {
    setting: 'Galatians 6:2 calls believers to bear one another’s burdens, while verse 5 also speaks of carrying one’s own load. Paul is teaching mutual responsibility within the church. Luke 5:15–16 describes Jesus withdrawing to pray amid growing demands; it is a narrative example rather than a universal schedule.',
    teaching: [
      'When a couple serves in recovery ministry, people may reach out at unpredictable hours with real needs. Compassion matters. So do limits. If every request becomes an emergency, the household may live in permanent interruption. The spouse who answers every call may become exhausted, while the other spouse quietly carries childcare, meals, and emotional strain. Neither outcome serves the family or the ministry well.',
      'Galatians holds two truths together. Christians help one another with heavy burdens, and people also carry appropriate personal responsibilities. We should not use “bear one another’s burdens” to make a single family responsible for everyone. Nor should “carry your own load” become an excuse to abandon someone in crisis. Wisdom asks what help is needed, who is equipped to provide it, and whether the responsibility should be shared.',
      'Jesus’ practice of withdrawing to pray reminds us that constant availability is not the same as faithfulness. Rest and prayer are not evidence of indifference. A ministry that depends on one person being reachable at all hours needs a stronger support structure. It is appropriate to build a rotating response team, provide crisis resources, and define what constitutes an immediate emergency.',
      'Together identify the requests you commonly receive. Sort them into urgent safety concerns, important but nonurgent needs, and requests that can be delegated. Agree on who should be contacted when you are unavailable. A serious crisis may require emergency services or qualified professionals rather than an untrained volunteer. Do not promise that your family can provide care beyond its capacity.',
      'Today practice saying a kind, clear boundary: “We care about you. We cannot meet tonight, but here is the next appropriate contact.” Boundaries work best when both spouses understand the plan and the people being served know where to turn. Healthy ministry can be compassionate without consuming a marriage.',
    ],
    questions: ['What kinds of requests most often interrupt our family time?', 'Which situations genuinely require immediate action?', 'Where have we confused being needed with being responsible?', 'What backup people or services can help?', 'How can we protect rest without abandoning someone in danger?', 'What boundary will we communicate this week?'],
    prayer: 'Lord, give us compassion and discernment. Help us carry burdens wisely, respect our limits, and build a ministry where care is shared rather than carried by one exhausted household. Amen.',
  },
  13: {
    setting: 'Deuteronomy 6:4–9 calls Israel to love the Lord and teach His commands diligently in daily household life. Ephesians 6:4 instructs fathers not to provoke their children but to bring them up with the Lord’s instruction. These passages support faithful caregiving; they do not prescribe instant closeness in blended families.',
    teaching: [
      'A blended family does not become secure simply because adults marry. Children may have experienced many transitions and may carry their own questions about belonging, loyalty, routines, and authority. Some children welcome a new adult quickly; others need more time. Their pace should not be treated as rejection or spiritual failure. Stability is built through repeated experiences of care.',
      'Deuteronomy pictures faith taught in the ordinary rhythms of life: sitting at home, walking along the road, lying down, and getting up. The passage is about more than formal lessons. Children learn from how adults speak under stress, keep promises, apologize, and treat one another. Ephesians warns against provoking children, reminding caregivers that authority should not become harshness or humiliation.',
      'For a household with children from a previous relationship and a child shared together, fairness does not always mean identical treatment. Ages, histories, and needs differ. What matters is that every child knows they are valued and protected. A new baby can bring joy and also change routines and attention. Older children may need reassurance that they have not become less important.',
      'Choose a simple belonging ritual that does not depend on forced affection: a predictable meal, a weekly walk, a bedtime conversation, or a short family prayer where participation is invited rather than demanded. Ask each child what helps them feel included in age-appropriate ways. Do not insist that a child use a parental title or disclose private feelings before trust has grown.',
      'Adults should discuss discipline, schedules, and communication privately rather than making children responsible for adult disagreements. When mistakes happen, model repair. A child who sees a parent say, “I spoke harshly, and that was wrong,” learns something important about repentance. Today choose consistency over grand gestures.',
    ],
    questions: ['What might each child be experiencing in this season?', 'How can we show belonging without demanding instant closeness?', 'What household routine could provide predictability?', 'How should we handle disagreements about parenting away from the children?', 'What changes may the newborn bring for older children?', 'What one promise can we reliably keep this week?'],
    prayer: 'Father, You see every child in our care. Give us patience, wisdom, and tenderness. Help our home become a place of truthful love, stability, and welcome. Amen.',
  },
  14: {
    setting: 'Ecclesiastes 3:1–8 poetically describes the changing times and seasons of human life. Philippians 2:4 urges believers to attend to the interests of others. Neither passage gives medical guidance about pregnancy or postpartum recovery; practical planning should follow the family’s actual needs and qualified clinical advice.',
    teaching: [
      'A new baby changes a household before the baby arrives. Sleep, appointments, meals, transportation, ministry obligations, and the needs of older children all compete for attention. Even a joyful season can be physically demanding and emotionally unpredictable. The couple does not need a perfect plan, but they do need honest expectations and permission to change the plan when reality changes.',
      'Ecclesiastes reminds us that life has seasons. Faithfulness may look different during a period of birth and recovery than it did during an active ministry season. Philippians calls us to notice the needs of others, which may mean prioritizing rest, protecting medical appointments, and declining invitations. No spouse should treat exhaustion as a lack of faith or insist that service continue at the old pace.',
      'Make the invisible work visible. Who will prepare meals? Who will care for older children during appointments? How will each person get protected rest? Who handles visitors, groceries, school routines, and urgent ministry calls? Which trusted people can bring food or help with transportation? A written plan prevents one spouse from becoming the default person for every task simply because the work is not discussed.',
      'A postpartum plan is not medical treatment. Physical recovery, mental health, feeding decisions, and complications should be discussed with qualified clinicians. If a parent experiences concerning physical or emotional symptoms, seek professional advice promptly. Do not assume prayer or perseverance replaces medical care. Ministry friends can offer practical help while respecting privacy and the family’s choices.',
      'For the weekly meeting, bring the parts of your support plan you both want your mentor to know. Ask for concrete help rather than broad promises: “Could someone deliver dinner Tuesday?” or “Can a team leader cover our calls for two weeks?” Today’s exercise is an act of love: plan together for a season in which your capacity may be limited, and remember that caring for your family is meaningful service.',
    ],
    questions: ['What responsibilities will become harder after the baby arrives?', 'How will we protect rest and recovery without keeping score?', 'What do the older children need during this transition?', 'Which ministry duties can we pause or delegate?', 'Who can provide practical help, and what can we ask them to do?', 'What part of our plan should we revisit if circumstances change?'],
    prayer: 'Lord, thank You for the life and responsibilities before us. Give us wisdom to prepare, humility to ask for help, and compassion for one another in a demanding season. Amen.',
  },
  15: {
    setting: 'Psalm 34:18 celebrates the Lord’s nearness to the brokenhearted in a song of deliverance. Romans 12:2 calls believers to be transformed by the renewing of the mind in the context of offering their lives to God. Neither text promises instant recovery from trauma or suggests that a spouse can heal another person by demanding disclosure.',
    teaching: [
      'Marriage brings two histories into one household. Some experiences are joyful; others may have taught a person to stay guarded, expect rejection, or react quickly when they feel cornered. Recovery can involve learning new habits while old fears remain. This does not mean every disagreement is caused by trauma, and it does not mean a spouse should diagnose the other person. It means we can approach confusing reactions with humility and curiosity.',
      'Psalm 34 does not say that brokenhearted people must pretend to be whole. It declares that God is near to them. Romans speaks of transformation, but the passage does not give a timetable or a technique for healing. Spiritual growth can happen alongside counseling, medical care, recovery support, and patient relationships. Asking for qualified help is not a failure of faith.',
      'Consider a spouse who becomes anxious when plans change suddenly. The other spouse may see a simple scheduling issue, while the anxious spouse experiences uncertainty more intensely. A helpful conversation starts with the present: “What would make this change easier to handle?” It does not require a detailed account of painful events. Sometimes the most loving response is to make a plan, give advance notice, or allow time to settle.',
      'Each person is responsible for their own conduct even when feelings are strong. Pain can help explain a response, but it does not excuse intimidation or harm. Likewise, a partner is not responsible for becoming a therapist or absorbing mistreatment. Healthy support includes clear boundaries and respect for privacy. If either spouse feels unsafe, pause joint work and seek appropriate individual help.',
      'Today choose one ordinary grounding practice, such as taking slow breaths, naming what is happening now, stepping outside briefly, or contacting a trusted support person. Share only what you freely wish to share. Then identify one response you would like to practice differently the next time stress rises. Small changes are real changes.',
    ],
    questions: ['What reactions do I notice when I feel overwhelmed?', 'What is the difference between explaining a reaction and excusing harmful behavior?', 'What kind of support feels helpful rather than intrusive?', 'What personal history am I comfortable sharing, and what should remain private?', 'When would qualified outside support be wise?', 'What one response would I like to practice differently this week?'],
    prayer: 'Lord, You are near to the brokenhearted. Give us patience with the healing process, wisdom about our limits, and courage to seek the help we need. Teach us to respond with truth and compassion. Amen.',
  },
  16: {
    setting: 'Ephesians 4:31–32 calls believers to put away bitterness and practice kindness and forgiveness because of God’s forgiveness in Christ. Luke 17:3–4 includes confronting wrongdoing, repentance, and repeated forgiveness. These texts must not be used to erase accountability or demand immediate restoration of trust.',
    teaching: [
      '“I forgive you” and “I trust you again” are not the same statement. Forgiveness concerns how we respond to wrong and the call to release vengeance. Trust concerns whether someone has become reliable and safe. A spouse may sincerely desire to forgive while still needing boundaries, time, and evidence of change. That distinction is especially important when hurtful patterns have repeated.',
      'Paul’s words about kindness and forgiveness are not an instruction to pretend that harm did not happen. Jesus’ teaching in Luke includes naming wrongdoing. Honest repentance does not merely say, “Sorry you were upset.” It acknowledges the action, its effect, and what will be different. When wrongdoing is serious, accountability may involve pastoral oversight, qualified counseling, or protective action rather than a private promise.',
      'Imagine one spouse repeatedly commits to being home for a family responsibility and then cancels without warning. A sincere apology might be: “I said I would handle pickup, and I left you scrambling. That was wrong. I will put it on my calendar and arrange a backup before accepting other commitments.” The injured spouse can acknowledge the apology without being required to feel confident immediately. Reliability grows when the plan is actually followed.',
      'Forgiveness must never be weaponized: “If you were a good Christian, you would stop bringing this up.” Nor does reconciliation require staying in a threatening situation. If there is coercion, abuse, or fear, safety comes first. Joint exercises should stop, and individual professional or specialist support may be needed. A mentor should never promise confidentiality when someone is in immediate danger or when reporting obligations apply.',
      'For today, discuss a small, safe example of a broken commitment. Name what happened without exaggeration, identify an appropriate repair, and agree on how follow-through will be noticed. Do not use this exercise to process serious abuse or demand a declaration of forgiveness. The goal is to learn the shape of truthful repentance and patient rebuilding.',
    ],
    questions: ['How is forgiveness different from restored trust?', 'What makes an apology specific and accountable?', 'What behavior would demonstrate real change over time?', 'How can we discuss a repeated problem without demanding instant reassurance?', 'What boundaries are appropriate while trust is being rebuilt?', 'When should we seek outside help rather than handling an issue alone?'],
    prayer: 'Merciful God, teach us to repent truthfully and forgive without denying reality. Protect us from using Your Word to excuse harm. Help us pursue accountability, wisdom, and genuine change. Amen.',
  },
  17: {
    setting: 'Luke 10:38–42 recounts Martha’s hospitality and Mary’s attention to Jesus; Jesus addresses Martha’s anxiety and distraction rather than condemning all service. John 15:4–5 teaches disciples to abide in Christ as branches depend on the vine. The passages challenge frantic self-reliance, not responsible work or ministry.',
    teaching: [
      'Serving people can become deeply meaningful, especially when you have experienced recovery and want others to find hope. Yet it is possible to begin measuring your worth by how often people need you. A full phone, an urgent request, or a packed calendar can feel like proof that you matter. When the requests stop, you may feel restless or guilty. That is a difficult burden to place on yourself and on your family.',
      'Jesus did not tell Martha that hospitality was worthless. He pointed to what her worry and distraction were doing in that moment. John 15 goes deeper: fruitful life begins with remaining in Christ, not with trying to prove value through endless activity. A person can do generous work while neglecting prayer, rest, and the people closest to them. More activity is not always more faithfulness.',
      'In marriage, an identity built on being indispensable can make boundaries feel threatening. A spouse who asks for protected family time may be heard as an obstacle to God’s work. That interpretation deserves examination. A healthy ministry should have shared responsibility, trusted backup people, and room for volunteers to step away. Your spouse and children should not have to compete with a stream of avoidable emergencies.',
      'Try noticing the emotion that rises when you consider saying no. Is it compassion, fear of disappointing someone, fear of losing a role, or concern about how you will be seen? Do not shame yourself for the answer. Bring it honestly before Christ. Then ask what faithful service looks like at your current capacity, including the responsibilities of home.',
      'Today identify one request that can be delegated, delayed, or declined. Communicate the boundary kindly and make sure an appropriate alternative exists when needed. Use the reclaimed time for prayer, rest, or attentive presence with family. The goal is not withdrawal from ministry; it is ministry rooted in Christ rather than personal indispensability.',
    ],
    questions: ['When do I feel most valuable to other people?', 'What emotions arise when I consider saying no?', 'How can ministry service unintentionally compete with family responsibilities?', 'What does abiding in Christ look like in an ordinary week?', 'Who could share a responsibility I have been carrying alone?', 'What boundary would make our service more sustainable?'],
    prayer: 'Jesus, our worth comes from belonging to You. Free us from the need to be indispensable. Teach us to serve faithfully, rest wisely, and remain present with the people You have entrusted to us. Amen.',
  },
  18: {
    setting: 'Romans 12:9–10 calls believers to sincere love and mutual honor. Colossians 3:12–14 calls the church to compassion, kindness, humility, patience, forgiveness, and love. These virtues are incompatible with intimidation, humiliation, and coercive control; neither passage instructs a threatened person to remain in danger.',
    teaching: [
      'Emotional safety means being able to speak truthfully, disagree, and express a need without expecting threats, humiliation, punishment, or retaliation. It does not mean never feeling disappointed or uncomfortable. Healthy conversations can be difficult. The difference is that both people remain free to speak, pause, and set boundaries without fear.',
      'Scripture’s call to genuine love does not ask us to conceal harm behind religious language. Respect includes allowing the other person to have a different perspective. Compassion includes recognizing when someone is overwhelmed. Patience includes giving space for a conversation to resume later. Love is not proven by how much mistreatment a person can tolerate.',
      'A couple can create ordinary safety practices: no yelling over one another, no insults, no threats of abandonment, no use of private disclosures as weapons, and no quoting Scripture to force compliance. If either person requests a pause, the other respects it. These agreements help only when both people are genuinely free to participate and there is no pattern of intimidation.',
      'It is important not to treat all conflict as symmetrical. If one spouse fears what will happen after speaking honestly, a joint exercise or shared journal may increase risk. The appropriate next step is private, qualified support and a safety assessment, not a promise to communicate better. A ministry mentor should listen carefully, avoid pressuring disclosure, and follow applicable safeguarding obligations.',
      'If your relationship is safe enough for this exercise, each spouse can name two conditions that help them speak honestly and one respectful boundary. Listen without arguing over whether the feeling is justified. Then agree on one practice you can both sustain. Safety grows through consistent behavior, not through a document alone.',
    ],
    questions: ['What helps me express a concern without fear?', 'What is the difference between discomfort and intimidation?', 'How do we respect a request to pause?', 'What words or behaviors should never be part of our disagreements?', 'How can we avoid using Scripture or private information as leverage?', 'What outside support would we seek if either person felt unsafe?'],
    prayer: 'God of truth and mercy, teach us to honor one another with sincere love. Give us courage to reject intimidation, respect boundaries, and seek wise help when safety is uncertain. Amen.',
  },
  19: {
    setting: 'Philippians 4:6–7 encourages believers to bring concerns to God with prayer and thanksgiving, promising God’s peace to guard hearts and minds in Christ. First Peter 3:7 connects a husband’s treatment of his wife with his prayers. Neither text makes prayer a substitute for practical responsibility or permission to correct a spouse through public religious language.',
    teaching: [
      'Praying together can be deeply connecting, but it can also feel uncomfortable when a couple is learning to trust one another. One spouse may be used to praying aloud; the other may prefer silence or short sentences. Neither person should be embarrassed for not knowing what to say. Prayer is not a performance, and the length or polish of a prayer does not measure spiritual maturity.',
      'Paul invites believers to bring real needs to God. That includes fatigue, financial concerns, recovery responsibilities, family transitions, and difficult conversations. God’s peace is not a guarantee that every problem disappears. Prayer can steady us while we continue to make wise decisions, seek help, and carry out practical responsibilities.',
      'Peter reminds husbands that the way they treat their wives matters to their relationship with God. It is inconsistent to pray eloquently while dismissing or dishonoring a spouse. The point is not to weaponize prayer by saying, “God told me you must change.” A prayer such as “Lord, show my spouse how wrong they are” may sound spiritual but functions as criticism. Instead, ask permission to pray and name needs without assigning blame.',
      'Try a simple rhythm. First, each person names one thing they are thankful for. Next, each person may name one concern they would like prayer for, or may choose not to share. Finally, offer short prayers for wisdom, patience, provision, and care. If one spouse does not want to pray aloud, the other can pray briefly without pressuring participation.',
      'Prayer and action belong together. After praying for rest, discuss how to make rest possible. After praying for the children, choose a practical way to care for them. After praying for peace, address the issue that needs attention. Today the goal is to invite God into your shared life without using spiritual language to control one another.',
    ],
    questions: ['What has prayer felt like in our past experiences?', 'What would help us pray together without pressure?', 'How can a prayer become a disguised criticism?', 'What concern would I freely like prayer for this week?', 'What practical action should accompany that prayer?', 'What short prayer rhythm could we sustain during a busy season?'],
    prayer: 'Father, thank You that we can bring our needs to You. Give us peace, humility, and wisdom. Help our prayers lead to compassionate action and deeper trust in Christ. Amen.',
  },
  20: {
    setting: 'Matthew 7:24–27 concludes Jesus’ Sermon on the Mount with the contrast between hearing His words and doing them. Ecclesiastes 4:9–12 praises companionship and mutual help in adversity. The image of a strong foundation is about obedience to Jesus, not a promise that Christian couples will avoid hardship.',
    teaching: [
      'A marriage is rarely tested only by one dramatic event. More often, pressure comes through repeated ordinary demands: interrupted sleep, childcare, schedules, bills, ministry requests, and unfinished conversations. When people are tired, they tend to fall back on familiar habits. That is why a realistic rhythm matters more than an ambitious plan that lasts three days.',
      'Jesus’ picture of the wise builder makes an important distinction. Both builders hear; the wise one acts. Learning biblical principles is valuable, but the teaching becomes visible when spouses listen patiently, speak truthfully, keep commitments, and seek help when needed. Storms still come. The promise is not an effortless life but the wisdom of building on His words.',
      'Ecclesiastes reminds us that companionship can provide support when one person falls or grows cold. Yet support should not become dependency on a single person for every need. Healthy couples may also need church community, recovery support, medical professionals, and trusted friends. Marriage is a covenant relationship, not the only source of care.',
      'Choose three rhythms. A daily check-in might be ten minutes with phones away. A weekly planning conversation might cover children, ministry commitments, meals, and rest. A monthly review might ask what is working and what needs to change. Keep each practice small enough to survive a newborn’s arrival. If a day is missed, resume without treating the lapse as a moral failure.',
      'Build a backup plan for difficult weeks. Decide how to request a pause, who can help with childcare, and when to contact a qualified professional. Faithfulness is often found in returning to simple practices after interruptions. Today write a plan you could actually follow when you are tired, not an idealized plan for a perfect week.',
    ],
    questions: ['Which habits have helped us most during these lessons?', 'What tends to break down first when we are tired?', 'What does hearing and doing Jesus’ words look like at home?', 'What daily connection rhythm is realistic?', 'What weekly and monthly conversations should we schedule?', 'Who can support us when our own capacity is limited?'],
    prayer: 'Lord Jesus, teach us not only to hear Your words but to practice them. Give us perseverance in ordinary responsibilities and wisdom to build habits that endure difficult seasons. Amen.',
  },
  21: {
    setting: 'Joshua 24:15 is Joshua’s covenant appeal to Israel to serve the Lord rather than other gods. Colossians 3:12–17 describes the character and worship of a community formed by Christ. Applying these passages to a couple’s shared commitments is appropriate, but neither text is a marriage vow formula or a guarantee of instant change.',
    teaching: [
      'Twenty-one days can begin a new pattern, but they cannot resolve every wound, habit, or practical challenge. The value of this journey is not a completed checklist. It is the opportunity to see where God is calling each person to take responsibility and where the couple can make wise, measurable changes together. Growth may be uneven, especially during a demanding family season.',
      'Joshua’s challenge to serve the Lord asks for a real direction of life. Colossians shows what that direction looks like in relationships: compassion, humility, patience, forgiveness, love, gratitude, and the word of Christ dwelling richly among believers. These are not tools for scoring a spouse. Each person asks what obedience looks like in their own speech, priorities, and actions.',
      'Look back at the communication covenant from Day 7, the family and ministry plan from Day 14, and the practices you chose in the final week. What has actually changed? Where did you follow through? Where did you make a promise that was too broad? Celebrate specific progress without pretending that every issue is settled. If trust or safety remains uncertain, do not manufacture a happy ending for your mentor.',
      'Create a thirty-day plan with one personal commitment from each spouse and one shared goal. Good commitments can be observed: “I will ask before accepting a new evening ministry commitment,” or “We will spend ten minutes checking in three evenings a week.” Avoid vague pledges such as “I will never disappoint you again.” Set a date to review the plan and agree that adjustments are allowed as the baby and household needs change.',
      'For the final weekly mentor meeting, share only what you both freely choose to share. Ask for help with accountability, practical support, and appropriate referrals if needed. Remember that Christian discipleship is lifelong. The aim is not to prove you have arrived but to keep choosing truth, responsibility, grace, and Christ-centered love in the next ordinary day.',
    ],
    questions: ['What have I learned about my own patterns?', 'Where have I seen concrete progress rather than just good intentions?', 'What still needs time, accountability, or qualified support?', 'Which commitments from Days 7 and 14 should we keep or revise?', 'What personal change will each of us practice for thirty days?', 'When will we review our shared goal and ask for help if needed?'],
    prayer: 'Lord Jesus, thank You for meeting us in ordinary life. Give us humility to keep learning, courage to repair what we have harmed, and wisdom to serve You faithfully as a family. Keep our hope anchored in You. Amen.',
  },
};
